      "id": 1,
      "claim": "Typical kitchen aluminum foil (~16 µm thick) has a skin depth comparable to or larger than its thickness for most radio frequencies used in everyday communication, so a single layer provides only modest attenuation per pass.",
      "evidence": "Skin‑depth formula δ = sqrt(2ρ/ωμ) with ρ≈2.7×10⁻⁸ Ω·m for aluminum gives δ≈8 µm at 100 MHz and larger at lower frequencies; attenuation ≈ 3 dB per layer.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "A hat made of foil does not form a closed conductive enclosure; large gaps remain at the sides, back and bottom.",
      "evidence": "Geometric analysis of a typical cap shows coverage of <30 % of the solid angle around the head, leaving most incident fields unshielded.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Because of the limited material attenuation (Step 1) and the open geometry (Step 2), the overall shielding of ambient RF fields is modest (≈5–10 dB) across 30 MHz–3 GHz.",
      "evidence": "Empirical measurements of makeshift foil hats (MIT Media Lab, 2005; other university labs) report attenuation <10 dB over the whole commercial RF band; gaps dominate the shielding performance.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": [1, 2]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "No scientifically verified electromagnetic carrier exists for “mind‑control”, alien transmission, or thought‑reading signals in the frequency ranges that foil could affect.",
      "evidence": "Neuroscience shows brain activity is low‑frequency electric potentials not radiated as far‑field EM waves; literature contains no peer‑reviewed evidence of external mind‑control RF signals.",
      "weight": 0.15,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore a foil hat cannot block or enable reception of such non‑existent signals.",
      "evidence": "Combining modest attenuation (Step 3) with the absence of relevant signals (Step 4) yields no protective or receptive effect.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [3, 4]
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "claim": "Controlled experiments with participants wearing foil hats show no measurable change in EM exposure, cognition, EEG patterns, or subjective perception.",
      "evidence": "MIT Media Lab (2005) and follow‑up studies found no statistically significant differences between hat and no‑hat conditions.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "claim": "Options B–G each require either effective shielding/antenna (which does not exist per Steps 1‑3) or the existence of the claimed signals (which does not exist per Step 4).",
      "evidence": "Logical elimination: if the premises of B–G are false, the statements themselves are false.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [3, 4, 5, 6]
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "claim": "Since all specific alternatives are false, the only remaining consistent statement is that nothing in particular happens when you wear a tin‑foil hat.",
      "evidence": "Deductive closure of the previous steps.",
      "weight": 0.05,
      "depends_on": [7]
    }
